WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:09.020
Proteins headed for the cell surface fold inside the endoplasmic reticulum.

2
00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:15.300
If unfolded chains arrive faster than they can be folded, the cell needs to know.

3
00:00:16.280 --> 00:00:20.120
The unfolded protein response does this with three sensors.

4
00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:24.560
One is IRE1, which spans the membrane.

5
00:00:25.520 --> 00:00:29.880
When unfolded protein builds up, IRE1 molecules cluster.

6
00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:35.820
Clustering switches on an RNA-cutting activity in their cytoplasmic ends.

7
00:00:36.683 --> 00:00:43.183
In its best-known job, IRE1 cuts a segment out of one messenger RNA.

8
00:00:44.123 --> 00:00:47.783
In animals, that message is XBP1.

9
00:00:48.643 --> 00:00:50.643
A ligase then joins the ends.

10
00:00:51.423 --> 00:00:55.243
Only the spliced message yields an active transcription factor.

11
00:00:56.033 --> 00:00:58.553
Look at the piece that is removed.

12
00:00:59.453 --> 00:01:03.273
In XBP1 it is 26 nucleotides.

13
00:01:03.773 --> 00:01:05.873
That is not a multiple of three.

14
00:01:06.433 --> 00:01:10.513
So the reading frame shifts, and the protein gains a new tail.

15
00:01:11.417 --> 00:01:14.177
That factor orders more chaperones.

16
00:01:15.037 --> 00:01:18.697
Chaperones help proteins fold, so folding capacity rises.

17
00:01:19.557 --> 00:01:20.977
This is the repair branch.

18
00:01:21.597 --> 00:01:24.637
Keep this order. IRE1 clusters.

19
00:01:25.437 --> 00:01:27.257
The message is cut and joined.

20
00:01:27.877 --> 00:01:30.977
The frame shifts. An active factor appears.
